A||N||4||kornholio||transmission fluid leak||||1||
Z||000000||kornholio||04-19-2001||10:59 PM||||I've noticed that there is a small amount of transmission fluid leaking from under the driverside (right next to the coolant pipe I think).  I looked under the car and noticed that a small hose was covered in transmission fluid.  Does this little hose carry transmission fluid?  I haven't really taken a real close look at it.  If it is a transmission fluid hose, how hard is it to replace a leaking one?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||SteveJ||04-20-2001||09:59 AM||||Two steel tubes come off the automatic transmission and run about 8 to 10 inches where they connect to rubber hose, that compensates for engine movement. The rubber tube runs another 4 to 6 inches before connecting to steel tubing on the driver's side wheel well. This tubing runs to the radiator in the front where it plugs into the radiator to provide cooling for the transmission. ( In the winter it provides a quick warmup to the transmission that improves driveability )<P>These are your transmission cooling lines. You need to imediately check the transmission fluid level to insure you haven't lost too much due to the leak. If you run your transmission without adequate fluid you will destroy it.<P>You need to find the leak and patch it. If the tubes are really rusty you must replace them or at least the rusty section. If the rubber tube has failed, they are cheap. Get a coil of tubing in a blister pack and some clamps.<P>As to how hard it is, well it depends on where the leak is. If its the rubber tube, which is common, its easy. The steel tubing requires a tubing bender for about $10 and some patient time matching the original tube. If its just a small section of rust you can use a compression fitting to patch in new steel tubing. Don't use copper or aluminum tubing they can't take automotive vibration and the steel tubing bends easy enough.<P>Incidently autozone is now selling a coated type of steel tubing that prevents future rust.<p>[This message has been edited by SteveJ (edited 04-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||kornholio||04-20-2001||11:36 AM||||If it is the rubber hose that is leaking, is it possible to replace it without lifting the car?  Or will I need to get under the car.  The reason I ask is because I can see one end of the rubber hose with the clamp, but I don't know where the other end is connected to.  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000003||SteveJ||04-20-2001||11:58 AM||||I did both my rubber tubes last year from the top but I could see the entire tube from the top. I'm not sure why you can't see it all. Make another effort.<P>Generally a leak is at the highest point that is damp. Maybe you have to dry these tubes off with paper towels and then idle the car to see where it gets wet.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Fierowrecker||04-20-2001||06:25 PM||||Hey Korn!<BR>If you lay flat on your back, you will see where they go...<BR>I had one blow and it sprayed tranny fluid all over the tire, what a ride!!!!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
